  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon highlights the deep upper low \nover the western CONUS, with broad ridging in place across most of \nthe eastern CONUS. There are hints at a weak vorticity maximum \nmoving out onto the Central Plains ahead of the main upper low. This \nwill be a player our likely thunderstorm development this evening. \nAt the surface, high pressure over the Northern Plains has helped to \npush a frontal boundary southward overnight into the area. This \nfront has stalled along a roughly McPherson to Topeka line, and has \neven pushed a bit back north here in Topeka over the past couple \nhours. Along and south of the boundary we remain very warm and \nhumid, with dewpoints in the low 70s and temperatures approaching 90 \nin spots. This is allowing ML CAPE to climb above 2000 J/kg. Coupled \nwith strong effective shear of around 45 kts, and the ingredients \nare in place for severe storms. Convergence along the front will \ngradually increase over the afternoon, supporting storm development \ninitially to our west in central KS by mid/late afternoon and \ntowards Topeka by 5-6 PM. The strong effective shear and steep mid-\nlevel lapse rates will be favorable for initial supercells capable \nof producing hail up to around golf ball size. However boundary \nparallel shear vectors should favor upscale growth with time, \ntrending the main hazards more towards damaging winds and heavy \nrain. Still can't completely rule out a brief tornado, especially \ntowards sunset with any supercell riding the boundary. However the \noverall threat looks rather limited, given a very short window \nbetween a more discrete storm mode earlier in the evening and better \nlow-level shear later in the evening. The severe threat should \ngradually taper off mid to late evening as instability weakens, but \na heavy rain and flash flooding threat may linger overnight given \nnear record PWAT for mid October and potential for localized \ntraining of thunderstorms.\n\nIndications are still that we'll see a bit of a break in rain \ncoverage Wednesday before the main upper low approaches Wednesday \nnight and Thursday. A few scattered showers may linger during the \nday, but for the most part it looks cool and cloudy on the north \nside of the front. As the upper low does approach Wednesday night, \nguidance is generally split into two camps for the potential second \nround of rain. On one end, some guidance (including the AI versions \nof both the GFS and ECMWF) keeps the surface low weaker and farther \nsouth across southeast Kansas. This would keep the better isentropic \nascent to our south, along with the heaviest rain. Most of northeast \nKansas would still see some rain, but lighter amounts and rates. \nAlternatively some guidance, particularly the global models, lift a \nstronger surface low farther north through northeast Kansas. This \nwould allow stronger WAA and isentropic ascent across east-central \nKS, supporting another swath of heavy rain, potentially 1-3\" in \nspots. May still need to extend the Flood Watch through Thursday \nmorning, but given the uncertainty have decided to hold off for now. \nLeaning towards the lighter scenario anyway, given how convectively \ninfluenced boundaries like this often behave.\n\nOnce the surface low does pull off to the east Thursday evening, \nmuch cooler and drier air will be ushered in behind the trailing \ncold front. Strong CAA should bring temperatures into the low/mid \n40s by Friday morning, and keep Friday highs in the 50s despite \nplenty of sun. By Saturday morning, strong high pressure will be \nlocated nearly overhead. Clear skies and light winds will promote \nnear-ideal radiational cooling, which may allow for some of our \nfirst frost of the season as temperatures fall into the low/mid 30s. \nReturn flow sets up for the latter part of the weekend as the high \ndeparts to our east. This will result in a gradual warming trend for \nthe end of the forecast period, with highs climbing back into the \n70s for early next week.",
